1
00:00:00,451 --> 00:00:02,076
Every agent reports success.

2
00:00:02,076 --> 00:00:03,526
Not every agent is right.

3
00:00:03,526 --> 00:00:10,256
An investigation can find out why something failed, but the lesson usually stays trapped in the report it came from.

4
00:00:10,256 --> 00:00:21,628
AFTERMATH is failure prevention that remembers: it turns incident evidence into a safeguard, verifies it, and preserves it on Walrus Memory so the next similar failure is recognised, not repeated.

5
00:00:21,654 --> 00:00:27,342
This is the real, deployed product, and it is public: no account, no invitation, no approval.

6
00:00:27,342 --> 00:00:34,864
The three samples make exactly one claim, that the transaction completed successfully, over three real Sui Mainnet digests.

7
00:00:34,864 --> 00:00:36,332
Only the digest differs.

8
00:00:36,332 --> 00:00:41,286
So only one thing can change the verdict: what the Sui ledger says through Tatum.

9
00:00:41,289 --> 00:00:43,128
First, the agent claims success.

10
00:00:43,128 --> 00:00:48,529
AFTERMATH reads the digest on Sui Mainnet through Tatum, and Tatum reports a verified failure.

11
00:00:48,529 --> 00:00:50,311
The success claim cannot stand.

12
00:00:50,311 --> 00:00:56,057
The decision is block, and the evidence is grounded in the ledger rather than the agent's narration.

13
00:01:01,443 --> 00:01:04,447
The same claim again, but this digest did succeed.

14
00:01:04,447 --> 00:01:07,452
Tatum confirms it, and AFTERMATH allows the claim.

15
00:01:07,452 --> 00:01:10,096
Same words, same pipeline, opposite outcome.

16
00:01:10,096 --> 00:01:13,761
The verdict follows the authoritative source, not the report.

17
00:01:13,763 --> 00:01:17,711
A digest the node holds no record of is neither success nor failure.

18
00:01:17,711 --> 00:01:19,278
AFTERMATH refuses to guess.

19
00:01:19,278 --> 00:01:25,025
It returns require verification and states the uncertainty plainly, instead of inventing an answer.

20
00:01:29,766 --> 00:01:33,119
Verified safeguards are written to Walrus Memory on Mainnet.

21
00:01:33,119 --> 00:01:42,117
This ledger reads live from the relayer, and a write counts as stored only when a blob id comes back, and as recall-verified only when a recall query returns it.

22
00:01:42,117 --> 00:01:43,570
Nothing here is simulated.

23
00:01:43,576 --> 00:01:45,367
Here is the moment that matters.

24
00:01:45,367 --> 00:01:54,879
A safeguard learned from an unsupported completion claim was written to Walrus Memory, then recalled in later investigations, where it added required verification checks.

25
00:01:54,879 --> 00:02:02,936
The before and after are recorded honestly: the checks were strengthened, and AFTERMATH does not overclaim a verdict change that did not happen.

26
00:02:02,936 --> 00:02:08,979
Provenance shows the chain from the incident, to the Walrus blob, to the later investigations it influenced.

27
00:02:08,994 --> 00:02:13,635
AFTERMATH stays honest about what it knows, what it remembers, and what it changed.

28
00:02:13,635 --> 00:02:20,791
It does not merely remember that something happened; it makes the next investigation check what the last one taught us to check.

29
00:02:20,791 --> 00:02:22,972
Every failure should leave a safeguard.

